  "name": "Keroncong asli (Indonesian kroncong classic form)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Urban Indonesian song style descended from 16th-century Portuguese-Eurasian moresco fado brought by Mardijker communities to Batavia, standardised in the early 20th century as the 28-bar keroncong asli form by Jakarta and Solo ensembles and popularised on radio by Gesang and Waldjinah — a slow to moderate 4/4 at 60–90 BPM in which cak (high ukulele) plays offbeat strums, cuk answers on the beat, cello is plucked pizzicato as a kendang substitute, double bass walks sparse roots and violin and flute weave heterophonic countermelody under a light, straight-toned vocal with slow terminal vibrato.",
